th a molecule man Owen Reese but for  George Talton himself and really just  for advanced idea mechanics because they  wanted to be able to create a Cosmic  Cube and then study the Cosmic Cube to  understand its power the effects of a  cube could be far-reaching they didn't  quite know what would happen so what  they did is they transformed George  Tarleton into Modoc which initially  stood for mental organism designed only  for computing the problem with this is  that as his Cranium increased in size  and he became more intelligent his  ambition increased as well and what it  led to was Modoc basically turning on  Advanced idea mechanics seizing control  of the organization and then renaming  himself mental organism designed only  for killing and just kind of went  forward from there now after his debut  in Tales of suspense issue number 93  Modoc quickly became a recurring villain  in multiple Marvel comic book stories  like Captain America he also appeared in  Iron Man issue 74-75 as well as the cree  scroll War story line and Avengers  issues 89-97 although admittedly  everybody was in that story now one of  the important things about Modoc and  this is something to grasp while his  character was shown as exceedingly  dangerous and very very significant in  the older stories as time progressed  that began to change now this is  something we've talked about before more  in Marvel Comics when you had villains  that Rose to prominence in the 1960s and  70s that either Marvel didn't really  know what to do with them so you ended  up seeing them just kind of bouncing  around the landscape and then ultimately  disappearing entirely or Marvel would  experience a paradigm shift in terms of  how their characters were being handled  in the case of Modoc it was more of a  Marvel Paradigm Shift when you looked at  stories like the Incredible Hulk issue  number 168 in 1973 by Steve ingleheart  and Herb trimp this is one of those  times when Modoc was given to us as an  excee